course: french
lesson: 10
title: Days of the week and months
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most French days are named after the Moon and the planets: lundi is the Moon's day, mardi is Mars's day, mercredi Mercury's, jeudi Jupiter's and vendredi Venus's.
explain: English hides old gods in its days too: Monday is the Moon's day and Thursday is Thor's day.
explain: In French, days and months are written with a small letter, so you write lundi and janvier, not Lundi and Janvier, unless they start a sentence.
explain: A French calendar starts the week on lundi; picture Liam reading it as if Sunday came first and turning up for football a day late.
explain: Picture Léa in Marseille: le mercredi, je n'ai pas école, "on Wednesdays I don't have school", because many French primary schools have no lessons on Wednesday.
fact: The days are lundi (Monday), mardi (Tuesday), mercredi (Wednesday), jeudi (Thursday), vendredi (Friday), samedi (Saturday), dimanche (Sunday).
fact: lundi comes from the Moon, mardi from Mars, mercredi from Mercury, jeudi from Jupiter and vendredi from Venus.
fact: Days and months are written with a small letter in French unless they start a sentence.
fact: Many French primary schools have no lessons on Wednesday, and children do clubs or sport; le mercredi (with le) means "on Wednesdays, every week".
fact: Une semaine ("un suh-MEN") is a week; French calendars start the week on lundi.
fact: The months are janvier, février, mars, avril, mai, juin, juillet, août, septembre, octobre, novembre, décembre.
fact: août (August) sounds like "OOT" or "OO"; the a is silent and the little hat sits on the u.
fact: juin ("ZHWAN") is June and juillet ("zhwee-YAY") is July: the shorter word is the earlier month.
fact: "In" a month is en: en mai (in May), en janvier; you can also say au mois de mai.
fact: Quel jour sommes-nous ? means "What day is it?"; the answer is Nous sommes mardi, or, more casually, On est mardi.
fact: hier ("YAIR") is yesterday, aujourd'hui ("oh-zhoor-DWEE") is today and demain ("duh-MAN") is tomorrow; aujourd'hui is one word with an apostrophe, and long ago it meant, word for word, "on the day of today".
fact: In France the weekend is le week-end, with a hyphen; in Quebec people often say la fin de semaine.
fact: In the old Roman calendar the year began in March, so September (sept = 7) was the seventh month, October (oct = 8) the eighth, November (nov = 9) the ninth and December (déc = 10) the tenth.
fact: Slang here: "lowkey" means a bit; "no cap" means honestly; "well wonky" (UK) means very odd; "heaps" (Australia) means lots of; "dead" (Liverpool) means very; "la" (Liverpool) means mate; "tamping" (Wales) means fuming; "wicked" (Vermont) means very; "sus" means odd.
exercise: How should Holly write Monday in the middle of a French sentence? | lundi | Lundi | LUNDI | Lundy
exercise: Which planet gives its name to mardi? | Mars | Mercury | the Moon | Venus
exercise: Which day is named after the Moon? | lundi | dimanche | samedi | jeudi
exercise: What does vendredi mean? | Friday | Wednesday | Sunday | Saturday
exercise: What does Le mercredi, je n'ai pas école mean? | On Wednesdays I don't have school | This Wednesday I'm skipping school | On Wednesdays I go to school early | I never liked Wednesdays
exercise: On a French calendar, which day is in the first column? | lundi | dimanche | samedi | mercredi
exercise: What is une semaine? | a week | a weekend | a month | a seven
exercise: Which month comes right after juillet? | août | juin | mai | septembre
exercise: How do you say août? | "OOT" or "OO" | "a-OOT" | "OWT" | "AH-oo-tuh"
exercise: Which month is June? | juin | juillet | jeudi | janvier
exercise: How do you say "in May"? | en mai | dans mai | à mai | le mai
exercise: Poppy is asked "Quel jour sommes-nous ?" What is a good answer? | On est mardi. | Le chat. | Il est mardi. | J'ai mardi ans.
exercise: What does demain mean? | tomorrow | yesterday | today | the day after tomorrow
exercise: How do you write "today" in French? | aujourd'hui | aujourdhui | au jourd'hui | aujourd hui
exercise: What do people in Quebec often call the weekend? | la fin de semaine | le week-end anglais | la semaine finie | le samedi-dimanche
exercise: Why is septembre the 9th month though sept means 7? | The old Roman year began in March | The French added two months by mistake | It was the 7th month in Greece | September was moved by a king to be fair
test: Holly says, "But it's a day! Days get capitals!" What is the French rule? | Days and months take a small letter unless they start a sentence | Days take a capital letter, but months are always written small, as in English | Only lundi and dimanche take capitals | Days take capitals only in Quebec
test: Josh says, "Lundi is like Luna, the Moon, so Monday is Moon-day too. That's lowkey genius." ("Lowkey" means a bit.) Is he right? | Yes, both are named after the Moon | No, lundi is named after Mars | No, Monday is named after Thor | Yes, but only because French borrowed the word from English
test: Aisha replies, "No school on a Wednesday?! Lucky you, no cap." ("No cap" means honestly.) What is true? | Many French primary schools have no lessons on Wednesday | French children never go to school at all | Léa was skipping school without telling anyone | French schools are closed every day but Wednesday
test: Liam says the French week "starting on lundi is well wonky." ("Well wonky" means very odd.) What could happen if he forgets? | He could read the calendar wrong and turn up a day late | Nothing, the week starts on Sunday in France | The calendar would have eight days | He would arrive a week early
test: Ella sings the months and gets stuck: "That word's got heaps of letters and no sound." ("Heaps" means lots of.) Which month comes next after juillet? | août, said "OOT" | aout, said "a-OOT" | août, said "OWT" | aôut, said "OO-ah"
test: Archie says juin and juillet are "dead similar, la." ("Dead" means very.) Which trick helps? | The shorter word is the earlier month: juin is June | The longer word is the earlier month: juillet is June | juin is always in winter | They are the same month, two spellings
test: Jacob writes "dans mai". His teacher writes "en mai". He goes, "Y'all have a special word for that?" Which is right? | en mai, or au mois de mai | dans le mai | à le mai | en le mai
test: Poppy answers "Le chat" when asked the day. Her friend whispers, "Not chat, day!" What would a French kid say? | On est mardi. | C'est jour mardi. | Il fait mardi. | Je suis mardi.
test: Daniel says, "Aujourd'hui is so long it's tamping." ("Tamping" means fuming.) What did aujourd'hui once mean word for word? | on the day of today | today is a good day | all of the day | the day after
test: Olivia in Vermont says "Wait, you just use our word? That's wicked funny." ("Wicked" means very.) How is it written in France? | le week-end | le weekend | la weekend | le wiikend
test: Sam's mate says, "Sept is seven, so why's September the ninth month? That's sus." ("Sus" means odd.) What's the answer? | Long ago the Roman year began in March, so it was the 7th | It's a mistake that nobody ever fixed | September has seven weeks in it | Sept means nine in Latin
test: ★★★ Today is jeudi. What day was it hier, and what day will it be après-demain (the day after tomorrow)? | mercredi and samedi | vendredi and dimanche | mercredi and vendredi | mardi and samedi
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the orthography of the days and months in French? | They are written with a lower-case initial letter except at the commencement of a sentence | They are invariably written with an upper-case initial letter | They are written entirely in capitals in formal correspondence | They are written with an upper-case letter solely on weekdays
exam: Identify the celestial body from which the name jeudi is derived. | Jupiter | Venus | the Moon | Mars
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the month of août. | It is the eighth month and is pronounced "OOT" or "OO" | It is the seventh month and is pronounced "a-OOT" | It is the sixth month and is pronounced "OWT" | It is the ninth month and is pronounced "AH-oo"
exam: It is given that the old Roman year commenced in March. Ascertain the position of the month bearing the prefix oct- in that calendar. | Eighth | Tenth | Sixth | Twelfth
